# No Runtime Reflection

Do **not** use reflection in runtime code. Under IL2CPP + managed stripping (our WebGL builds), reflection is slow, allocates heavily, and silently breaks: stripped members return null, and AOT can't generate code for types discovered at runtime.

## Banned in runtime code

- `GetType().GetMethod(...)` / `GetProperty` / `GetField` / `Invoke`
- `Activator.CreateInstance(...)`
- `typeof(T).GetFields()` / attribute scanning at runtime (`GetCustomAttributes`)
- `Assembly.GetTypes()` / type discovery loops
- `Type.GetType("SomeClass, SomeAssembly")` string-based type lookup
- `dynamic`
- `Enum.GetValues` / `Enum.GetNames` / `enum.ToString()` in hot paths (reflection-backed and allocating — cache the results once)

```csharp
// ❌ BAD — runtime type discovery; breaks under stripping
var handler = Activator.CreateInstance(Type.GetType($"Game.{name}Handler"));
var method = target.GetType().GetMethod("OnHit");
method.Invoke(target, null);

// ✅ GOOD — explicit, direct dispatch
switch (kind)
{
    case EffectKind.Burn:
        ApplyBurn(target);
        break;
    case EffectKind.Freeze:
        ApplyFreeze(target);
        break;
    default:
        throw new ArgumentOutOfRangeException(nameof(kind), kind, "[EffectSystem] Apply — unhandled effect kind");
}
```

If you feel the need for reflection-based dispatch, the answer is an explicit `switch` on an enum, a `Dictionary<TKey, Action<T>>` populated with **statically referenced** methods, or plain direct calls.

## Allowed exceptions

- **Editor code** — inspectors, code generators, asset pipelines.
- **One-time serialization setup** at bootstrap (e.g. JSON converter registration) where the types are statically referenced so stripping keeps them — never per-frame or per-message.
- Third-party libraries that use reflection internally (e.g. Newtonsoft) — but keep our call sites typed, and prefer explicit `[JsonProperty]` contracts over convention-based discovery.

## Agent checklist

- [ ] No `Activator`, `GetMethod`/`Invoke`, `Assembly.GetTypes`, string-based `Type.GetType` in runtime code
- [ ] Dispatch uses `switch`/dictionary with statically referenced methods
- [ ] Enum names/values cached once, not queried per frame
- [ ] Any reflection is editor-only or documented one-time bootstrap
